    "product_id": "201d2c3f-4843-440a-9564-669d2202cbef",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 221751\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1051 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- The overall pattern will favor dry and tranquil conditions \n  across the Desert Southwest through at least the Thanksgiving \n  Weekend.\n\n- Temperatures over the next few days will hover near to slightly\n  below normal before a warming trend pushes readings toward \n  above seasonal levels by the back half of the upcoming week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nMorning objective analysis reveals a low amplitude pattern stretched \nout across the southwestern CONUS, a setup that will keep quiet \nconditions in place over the forecast area through the start of \nthe upcoming week. Broad troughing over the eastern Pacific will \nthrow some high-level cloud cover toward the region during the \nnext few days, but that will be the only \"noticeable\" change in \nconditions during this timeframe. Temperatures will continue their\nrun of near to below normal levels as the above-mentioned long- \nwave, and an eventual break-away piece of energy, will help to \nkeep 500mb height anomalies just below climatological normals. \nUpper 60s to low 70s will be the going rate for the lower \ndeserts, with high terrain readings in the 50s and 60s, the next \nfew afternoons.\n\nA quick shift in the pattern is expected for Tuesday, but not in a \nway that will result in any appreciable weather changes. Upper-level \nridging will quickly fill in over western CONUS, splitting the \nPacific trough and aforementioned short-wave that, by this point,\nwill have migrated over the Southern Plains. This will result in \nwarming of the atmospheric column, allowing day-to-day \ntemperatures to rise, but only by few degrees across the board. \nLower 70s will become more common for lower desert locales, with \n50s and 60s remaining in place for the higher terrain. \n  \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH NEXT SUNDAY/...  \nThe building ridge Tuesday will set the stage for a gradual warming \ntrend through the remainder of the week and into the weekend as anti-\ncyclonic curvature dominates during much of this timeframe. Global \nensembles do show another weak shortwave moving off the eastern \nPacific by Wednesday, likely putting a short pause on the daily \npositive temperature trajectory, but that will quickly be replaced \nwith what is shown to be an unseasonably strong area of high \npressure setting up west of the Baja Peninsula. Afternoon highs by \nThanksgiving will rise far enough to be comfortably above normals \nvalues for the back-end of November, but that will only result in \nmiddle to upper 70s becoming more common across lower elevation \nareas so conditions should still feel quite pleasant. There might be \na few spots, mainly out in SW Arizona and SE California, that reach \ninto the lower 80s by the weekend due to their closer proximity to \nthe greatest height anomalies, but that appears to be the upper \nlimit of how far temperatures will rise. With the high centered to \nour southwest, cool northerly flow will likely be an inhibiting \nfactor as to why we will not see warmth that is completely out of \nthe ordinary for this time of year. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1750Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo weather issues will exist through Monday under FEW to SCT mid\nand high clouds. Confidence is very good that light east winds\nwill be maintained through late afternoon before attempting to\nswitch to westerly around sunset. Extended periods of variable\ndirections or calm conditions will be common. A similar wind\npattern can be expected for Monday.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather issues will exist through Monday under high cirrus \ndecks. Extended periods of nearly calm conditions will be common \nwith a north component generally favored at KBLH and a west \ntrajectory at KIPL. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry conditions will be common across the region through the next \nseveral days. MinRHs are expected to range generally between \n15-30% through most of the forecast period, with cool overnight \nlows resulting in decent to good overnight recovery. Outside of \nperiods of marginally breezy conditions over high terrain areas \nand the Lower Colorado River Valley, light winds will be favored \nelsewhere. Temperatures will hover near to slightly below normal \nlevels through the front half of the upcoming week, ahead of \nslight warming later in the forecast period.\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...Updated 11/22 0910\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area due to continued water releases upwards \nof 3000 cfs. Water levels near Dateland continue to rise, with \ngauge data now showing a depth of around 7 ft and flow approaching\n900 cfs. CBRFC's latest forecast for this station has water \nrising above action stage (8 ft) by late this morning. A separate \nFlood Warning segment remains in effect from Dateland to Yuma. \nThese water levels will have the potential to flood farm fields \nand low water crossings along the river downstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...RW\nLONG TERM...RW \nAVIATION...18/Benedict \nFIRE WEATHER...RW \nHYDROLOGY...Benedict/RW\n",
